.TH SHRED 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
shred \- overwrite and optionally remove files to hide their contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shred
.RB [ \-fuvxz ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR size ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shred
overwrites each
.I file
in place, so that its contents are hard to recover, and with
.B \-u
removes it.
.PP
A pass writes random bytes from the start of the file, as many bytes as
the file is long, and then flushes the file to disk.
There are 3 passes unless
.B \-n
says otherwise.
With
.BR \-z ,
a final pass writes zeros.
The random bytes come from the Windows generator
.BR RtlGenRandom .
.PP
Files are processed in order.
The first file that cannot be opened or removed ends the run with an
error: earlier files stay shredded, and later ones are not touched.
A directory cannot be opened.
An operand of
.B \-
names a file, not standard input or output.
.PP
A file of length 0 is not written, whatever
.B \-s
says, but
.B \-u
still removes it.
With
.BR \-u ,
after the last pass
.B shred
truncates the file to length 0, closes it, and removes it.
The name is not changed first.
.PP
.B shred
prints nothing when it succeeds.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-zu ,
and an option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-n5 ,
or be the next argument.
Options may follow the
.I file
operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options except
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-f
If a file cannot be opened for writing, clear its read-only attribute,
as
.B "chmod 666"
does, and try again.
The attribute stays cleared even if the file still cannot be opened,
as with a directory or a file that an access control list protects.
.TP
.BI "\-s " size
Write
.I size
bytes in each pass, instead of the length of the file.
This applies to the
.B \-z
pass too.
.I size
is decimal, hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or octal with a leading
.BR 0 .
It takes no suffix such as
.BR K .
A
.I size
beyond the end of the file makes the file grow, and a
.I size
of 0 writes nothing.
.IP
An invalid
.IR size ,
such as one that is negative, has leading white space, or is followed by
other text, prints the usage message on standard error and exits with
status 1.
It is not examined for a file of length 0.
.TP
.BI "\-n " count
Make
.I count
passes of random bytes instead of 3.
.I count
is a decimal number from 0 to 2147483647.
With 0, no random pass is made, and
.B shred
writes only the
.B \-z
pass, if requested.
.TP
.B \-z
After the random passes, make a final pass that writes zeros.
.TP
.B \-u
Remove each file after overwriting it.
A read-only file cannot be opened without
.BR \-f ,
and so is left in place.
.TP
.B \-v
Accepted and ignored.
There is no progress output.
.TP
.B \-x
Accepted and ignored.
The length is never rounded up to a multiple of a block.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that busybox-w32 reads in every applet, such as those that
control console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was processed.
A read or write error during a pass is reported on standard error, but
does not stop
.B shred
or change the status.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error; an invalid
.I count
or
.IR size ;
a file that cannot be opened or removed; or a failure to rewind or close
a file.
.B shred
stops at the first such failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Overwrite a file, zero it, and remove it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shred \-zu secrets.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overwrite a file once, and keep it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shred \-n 1 notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overwrite and remove a read-only file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shred \-fu locked.dat
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overwrite the first mebibyte of a large file with one random pass:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shred \-n 1 \-s 0x100000 backup.img
.EE
.RE
.PP
Overwrite and remove every temporary file below the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.tmp\(aq \-exec shred \-u {} +
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR shred :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, and so no
.B \-\-random\-source
or
.BR \-\-remove .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-s
takes no suffixes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
and
.B \-x
do nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
The first file that cannot be opened or removed ends the run, and a
read or write error does not change the exit status.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-u
removes a file without first renaming it.
.PP
.B shred
overwrites a file's data through the file system, so it cannot erase
copies kept elsewhere, such as in backups, shadow copies, and the page
file, or old blocks that a solid-state drive or a copy-on-write file
system retains after a write.
.PP
A hard link shares its data with the file's other names.
The overwrite shows through all of them, and
.B \-u
truncates the data to length 0 while removing only the name given.
NTFS alternate data streams are not overwritten.
.PP
Paths may use a drive letter and either kind of slash.
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.PP
An operand of
.I /dev/urandom
or
.I /dev/zero
is the null device, and it breaks the passes for the files after it:
after
.IR /dev/urandom ,
the random passes write nothing, and after
.IR /dev/zero ,
the
.B \-z
pass writes nothing.
The exit status is still 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR chmod (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR rm (1)
